%I A399720 #6 Sep 14 2026 00:06:29
%S A399720 1,3,8,22,60,168
%N A399720 Least N such that {1,...,N} contains an n-element subset whose set of subset sums contains no 3-term arithmetic progression (the Erdős-Sárközy function g_3(n)).
%C A399720 A set A = {a_1,...,a_n} of positive integers qualifies iff the 3^n sums Sum_{i=1..n} c_i*a_i with c_i in {0,1,2} are pairwise distinct (Korsky 2026, Proposition 4.1); equivalently, iff no nonzero c in {-2,...,2}^n has Sum_{i=1..n} c_i*a_i = 0. So this is the base-3 analog of the distinct-subset-sums condition of A276661.
%C A399720 If A qualifies for n then {1} U 3A qualifies for n+1, hence a(n+1) <= 3*a(n) and a(n)/3^n is nonincreasing.
%C A399720 Korsky (2026) proves a(n) >= (T(n)-1)/2 + Sum_{j=0..n-1} T(j), where T = A002426; this bound equals 1, 3, 8, 21, 56, 152, 419, ... and is attained exactly for n <= 3.
%C A399720 Costa (2026) proves liminf_{n->oo} a(n)/3^n = 0, answering negatively the question of Erdős and Sárközy whether a(n) >> 3^n (Erdős Problem #817); together with the monotonicity above this gives lim_{n->oo} a(n)/3^n = 0.
%C A399720 The powers of 3, A = {1, 3, ..., 3^(n-1)}, give a(n) <= 3^(n-1) and are optimal only for n <= 2. By monotonicity, a(n) <= (168/729)*3^n for all n >= 6.
%C A399720 Extremal sets: {1}; {1,3} and {2,3}; {5,7,8}; {7,19,21,22} and {14,19,21,22}; {19,52,57,59,60} and {38,52,57,59,60}; for n = 6, {107,145,159,162,166,168}. For n <= 5 these are all the extremal sets.
%C A399720 The first six terms coincide with terms of A318821 and A318863, which continue with 466; here 419 <= a(7) <= 504 (Korsky's bound and 3*a(6)), so a(7) = 466 is not excluded, but no connection between the sequences is known.
%H A399720 Thomas F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/817">Erdős Problem #817</a>, Erdős Problems website.
%H A399720 Simone Costa, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.06303">A negative answer to the Erdős-Sárkőzy question</a>, arXiv:2609.06303 [math.CO], 2026.
%H A399720 P. Erdős and A. Sárközy, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0012-365X(92)90119-Z">Arithmetic progressions in subset sums</a>, Discrete Math. 102 (1992), 249-264.
%H A399720 Samuel Korsky, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.24139">Arithmetic Progression-Free Subset-Sum Sets</a>, arXiv:2606.24139 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A399720 a(n+1) <= 3*a(n).
%F A399720 a(n) >= (A002426(n)-1)/2 + Sum_{j=0..n-1} A002426(j) (Korsky 2026).
%F A399720 lim_{n->oo} a(n)/3^n = 0 (Costa 2026, with the monotonicity above).
%e A399720 a(3) = 8: A = {5,7,8} has subset sums 0, 5, 7, 8, 12, 13, 15, 20, which contain no 3-term arithmetic progression, and no 3-element subset of {1,...,7} has this property. Equivalently, the 27 sums 5*c_1 + 7*c_2 + 8*c_3 with c_i in {0,1,2} are pairwise distinct.
%o A399720 (Python)
%o A399720 def a(n):
%o A399720     def rec(mask, cnt, lo, hi, k):  # partial ternary sums as a bitmask; adding x keeps them distinct iff the popcount triples
%o A399720         if k == 0: return True
%o A399720         for x in range(lo, hi - k + 2):
%o A399720             m = mask | (mask << x) | (mask << (2*x))
%o A399720             if m.bit_count() == 3*cnt and rec(m, 3*cnt, x + 1, hi, k - 1): return True
%o A399720         return False
%o A399720     N = n
%o A399720     while not rec(1 | (1 << N) | (1 << (2*N)), 3, 1, N - 1, n - 1): N += 1  # an extremal set must contain N
%o A399720     return N
%o A399720 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 6)])  # a(6) = 168 needs a search with stronger pruning
%Y A399720 Cf. A276661 (distinct subset sums: the base-2 analog), A005318, A002426, A003002, A318821, A318863.
%K A399720 nonn,hard,more,new
